course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 17
title: Marks, exercise books, tests and holidays
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Sashko waves his exercise book in the air and shouts "dvanAdtsyat!" — twelve! In Ukrainian schools that is the very top mark, so you answer "krutO!".
explain: A "dvoyka", a two, is the opposite: "I got a two" means the test went badly.
explain: Packing the bag for school you will hear the things inside: pidrUchnyk (textbook), zOshyt (exercise book), ruchka (pen), olIvets (pencil) — and the bag itself, ryukzAk.
explain: Then come the moans every kid knows: "zavtra kontrOlna" (a test tomorrow), "nudno na urOtsi" (the lesson's boring), "ya ne zrobyv" (I didn't do it) and "daI spysAty" (let me copy).
explain: And the best word of all: kanIkuly, the school holidays, when the dzvinOk, the bell, rings for the last time and everyone shouts "urA!".
fact: "dvanAdtsyat!" means twelve, and said about a mark it means the best possible mark; a "dvoyka" (a two) is a bad mark.
fact: "pidrUchnyk" means textbook, and "zOshyt" means exercise book.
fact: "zOshyt" is stressed on the first part; the end sounds like a rude English word, so English kids giggle, but in Ukrainian it is a completely normal word.
fact: "ruchka" means pen and also "little hand", so "daI ruchku" can mean "give me a pen" or "give me your hand".
fact: "olIvets" means pencil.
fact: "ryukzAk" means backpack or school bag; "sumka" is any bag, and a mum may say "ne zabud ryukzak!", don't forget your bag.
fact: "kontrOlna" means a test, and kids often answer "zavtra kontrOlna" with "zhakh!", oh no!
fact: "dzvinOk" means the bell, and the "dz" at its start is one sound, like in "adze".
fact: "nudno na urOtsi" means "it's boring in the lesson"; you moan it to friends, never to the teacher.
fact: "ya ne zrobyv" means "I didn't do it" when a boy says it; a girl says "ya ne zrobyla".
fact: "spysAty" means to copy someone's answers; copying is cheating, and a friend may answer "ni, sam roby!", no, do it yourself.
fact: "kanIkuly" means the school holidays, and "skoro kanIkuly!" (holidays soon!) gets the answer "urA!".
exercise: Sashko shows his test and shouts "dvanAdtsyat!". How did it go? | Brilliantly, it is the top mark | Badly, it is a low mark | Just average, half marks | He failed and must retake it
exercise: Sashko mutters that he got a "dvoyka". How did his test go? | Badly | Very well | He got full marks | He missed the test
exercise: What do you say when Sashko shouts "dvanAdtsyat!"? | krutO! | ne plAch | ne zlYsya | proBAch
exercise: What is a "pidrUchnyk"? | A textbook | An exercise book | A pencil case | A long ruler
exercise: Sashko writes his sums in a "zOshyt". What is it? | An exercise book | A textbook | A whiteboard | A school bag for books
exercise: Sashko whispers "daI ruchku". What could he want? | A pen, or your hand | A pencil sharpener | Your lunch box | The answers to copy
exercise: What is an "olIvets"? | A pencil | A rubber, an eraser | A ballpoint pen | A crayon box
exercise: Sashko's mum calls "ne zabud ryukzAk!". What must he not forget? | His school bag | His coat | His textbook for maths | His keys
exercise: Sashko says "zavtra kontrOlna". What is happening tomorrow? | A test | A school trip | A holiday | A football match
exercise: Someone groans "zavtra kontrOlna!". What do kids usually answer? | zhakh! | urA! | krutO! | molodEts!
exercise: What is "dzvinOk"? | The bell | The door | The break | The register
exercise: Who would you say "nudno na urOtsi" to? | Your friends, quietly | The teacher, politely | The head teacher | Your friend's granny
exercise: A boy says "ya ne zrobyv". What does he mean? | I didn't do it | I don't know | I don't want to | I'm not ready yet
exercise: How would a girl say "I didn't do it"? | ya ne zrobyla | ya ne zrobyv | ya ne znAyu | ya ne rozuMIyu
exercise: A boy whispers "daI spysAty". What does he want? | To copy your answers | To borrow your pencil | To sit next to you | To read your book
exercise: Sashko shouts "skoro kanIkuly!". What is coming soon? | The school holidays | A test | His birthday | The bell for the long break
test: my mate says zOshyt is a rude word and Sashko said a swear in class lol. right? | No, it is just the word for exercise book | Yes, it is a rude word in Ukrainian too | Yes, but only teachers may say it | No, it is the word for teacher
test: Sashko got a 10 out of 12 and is gutted. Your mate laughs: "10 is amazing, why's he sad?". What is true? | 10 is a good mark, but 12 is the best | 10 is the top mark in Ukraine | 10 is a fail in Ukraine | Marks in Ukraine go up to 100
test: You ask "daI spysAty?" and Sashko says "ni, sam roby!". What did he say? | No, do it yourself | No, I lost my pen | Yes, take it all | No, it's home time
test: Sashko's mate is copying answers in the test. What is spysAty really? | Cheating | Teamwork the teacher allows | Revising for the test | Helping a friend learn
test: What would happen if you said "nudno na urOtsi" loudly to the teacher? | She would think you were being rude | She would give you a 12 | She would end the lesson early | Nothing, it means "thank you"
test: "the dz in dzvinOk is two sounds, d then z, innit". What's true? | It is one sound, like in "adze" | It is two separate sounds | The d is silent | The z is silent
test: In a cartoon, a Ukrainian kid hears "zavtra kontrOlna" and cheers "urA!". What would a real kid more likely say? | zhakh! | krutO! | DYAkuyu! | DOdomu!
test: Sashko holds up a big book with chapters and pictures that the school lends him. Which is it? | pidrUchnyk | zOshyt | olIvets | ryukzAk
test: Your sister says "sumka" and "ryukzAk" mean exactly the same. What's the difference? | ryukzAk is a backpack, sumka is any bag | sumka is a pencil case | ryukzAk is a lunch box | There is no difference at all
test: Last day of term, the dzvinOk rings and everyone yells "kanIkuly!". Why are they happy? | The holidays have started | They all got 12 in the test | The test is cancelled | The teacher is ill
test: Sashko's little sister says "ya ne zrobyv" about her drawing. What slip did she make? | A girl says "ya ne zrobyla" | She should say "ya ne znAyu" | Girls never say this phrase | It should be "ty ne zrobyv"
exam: It is given that a Ukrainian pupil reports a mark of "dvanAdtsyat". Ascertain the evaluation which the said mark denotes. | The highest attainable assessment | The lowest attainable assessment | A failing assessment requiring re-examination | A median assessment of average merit
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the term "spysAty" as employed among schoolchildren. | It denotes the copying of another pupil's answers, which constitutes cheating | It denotes the borrowing of a writing implement | It denotes the completion of homework | It denotes the ringing of the school bell
exam: Identify the item which the expression "zOshyt" designates within the context of a Ukrainian classroom. | An exercise book | A textbook | A backpack | An obscene expression
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "kanIkuly"? | It designates the period of school holidays | It designates a written examination at term's end | It designates the bell concluding a lesson | It designates the mark of twelve
